    title: 'A fixed run of values',
    subtitle: 'Declaring and indexing arrays',
    concept:
      'An array holds a fixed number of same-typed values. Two ways to create one:\n\n`int[] nums = new int[5];` allocates five slots, **zero-filled** \u2014 unlike C, Java always initialises them. Objects default to `null`, booleans to `false`.\n\n`int[] nums = {4, 8, 15};` uses a literal and infers the length.\n\nUnlike C, a Java array **knows its length**, exposed as the field `.length` \u2014 no parentheses, because it is a field rather than a method. And unlike C, going out of bounds throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` instead of silently corrupting memory.',
    example: 'public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] zeros = new int[4];\n        int[] nums = {4, 8, 15, 16};\n        String[] names = new String[2];\n\n        System.out.println(zeros[0]);\n        System.out.println(names[0]);\n        System.out.println(nums.length);\n        System.out.println(nums[3]);\n\n        nums[1] = 99;\n        System.out.println(nums[1]);\n\n        try {\n            System.out.println(nums[10]);\n        } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {\n            System.out.println("out of bounds");\n        }\n    }\n}',
    note: 'The out-of-bounds access threw an exception rather than returning rubbish \u2014 a major safety improvement over C.',
