    concept:
      '`int[][] grid = new int[3][4];` creates three rows of four columns, accessed as `grid[row][col]`.\n\nJava\u2019s two-dimensional arrays are genuinely **arrays of arrays**, so each row is a separate object with its own length. That means rows can differ in size \u2014 a **ragged** array \u2014 which a C two-dimensional array cannot do.\n\n`grid.length` gives the row count and `grid[r].length` the length of that row. Always use the latter in the inner loop, since it may vary.\n\n`Arrays.deepToString` prints a nested array readably, where `Arrays.toString` would show the inner references.',
    example: 'import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] grid = new int[2][3];\n\n        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) {\n            for (int c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++) {\n                grid[r][c] = r * c;\n            }\n        }\n\n        System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(grid));\n        for (int[] row : grid) System.out.println(Arrays.toString(row));\n\n        int[][] ragged = {{1}, {1, 2}, {1, 2, 3}};\n        for (int[] row : ragged) System.out.print(row.length + " ");\n        System.out.println();\n    }\n}',
